  implementation cycle; we'd brainstorm #1 properly now and leave the rest sketched.

  Before I go further, the question that resizes this whole thing:

  The fork: whether "users" means several real people sharing data, or one person on their own device. This is the
  single largest cost driver in the project, and it's the least reversible — it decides whether there's a server at all,
  and retrofitting multi-user onto a local-only app means rewriting persistence, adding auth, and redoing the data
  model.

  - [1mMulti-user, shared tasks[0m — buys you real collaboration and makes "tasks they care about" meaningful (someone else
    changed your task). Costs a backend, a database, auth, and hosting. Rules out staying a static page.
  - [1mSingle-user, one device[0m — buys you a working app in a fraction of the time; persistence is [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and there's
    no server. Costs: "notifications" can only mean reminders you set yourself (due dates), since nobody else can change
    your tasks. Cheap now, expensive to convert later.
  - [1mSingle-user, synced across devices[0m — middle ground; needs a backend but no auth complexity beyond one account.
    Notifications still means self-reminders.

  Recommendation: I can't recommend one without knowing the actual use case, and that's the point — the phrase "users
  get notified" reads as multi-user, but plenty of solo task apps say "notify me." If it's genuinely multi-user, say so
  and we'll plan for a server. If you're not sure, single-user is the cheaper mistake only if you're confident it won't
  need to be shared.
